@section Undo

@cindex undo
@cindex redo

Emacs keeps @strong{one linear sequence} of changes, and undo commands
are themselves recorded into it.  Redo is therefore emergent, not a
second stack.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .66
@item @kbd{Cmd-Z}, @kbd{C-/}, @kbd{C-_} @tab Undo.
@item @kbd{Cmd-Shift-Z}, @kbd{C-?} @tab Redo (@code{undo-redo}).
@item @kbd{C-x u}, @kbd{Cmd-Shift-U} @tab vundo — the history as a
navigable tree.
@item @kbd{C-g} then @kbd{C-/} @tab The classic manoeuvre: break the undo
run, so the next undo goes @emph{forward}.
@item Select a region, then undo @tab Undoes only changes inside it.
@end multitable

The payoff is that no state is ever unreachable.  In a two-stack editor,
undoing five steps and then typing destroys those five redo states
permanently; here they are still in the sequence, so vundo can walk back
to them.

