@node Compile and Diagnostics
@chapter Compile and Diagnostics

@cindex compile
@cindex diagnostics

Project diagnostics are emitted as @code{file:line:col: severity:
message} — the GNU format @code{compilation-mode} already parses.  So
after @kbd{C-c h b}, @kbd{C-c h t} or @kbd{C-c h l}:

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@item @kbd{M-g M-n} @tab Next error.
@item @kbd{M-g M-p} @tab Previous error.
@item @kbd{RET} @tab Jump to it.
@item @kbd{g} @tab Re-run.
@end multitable

@strong{The compile buffer is the authority} — it is the same check CI
runs.  Squiggles from eglot and eslint are the secondary, advisory
signal.

@node TypeScript
@chapter TypeScript

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .78
@item @kbd{C-c l a} @tab Code actions.
@item @kbd{C-c l r} @tab Rename symbol.
@item @kbd{C-c l d} @tab Documentation.
@item @kbd{C-c l i} @tab Find implementation.
@item @kbd{C-c l x} @tab @code{eslint --fix} this file.
@item @kbd{M-.} / @kbd{M-,} @tab Jump to definition / back.
@item @kbd{C-M-i} @tab Complete.
@end multitable

The server resolves TypeScript from the project's own
@file{node_modules}, so the editor and @command{tsc} are always the same
compiler.  The version-skew problem that a bundled TypeScript causes
cannot arise here.

@node Git
@chapter Git

@kbd{C-x g} opens magit status.  Then @kbd{s} stage, @kbd{c c} commit,
@kbd{P p} push, @kbd{b b} branch, @kbd{F p} pull, @kbd{l l} log, and
@kbd{?} to show every key.  Forge (@kbd{'} in magit) adds GitHub issues
and pull requests.

@kbd{C-c t} opens a vterm running bash — run @command{claude} there and
its output paths become clickable.

@node The Content Index
@chapter The Content Index

@cindex content index
@cindex index, content

Every content build walks the whole note tree and parses every note's
frontmatter, then throws the result away.  The content index publishes
that walk instead, so anything — you, this editor, a CI check, another
package's build — can ask a question about the content without
re-deriving it.

It is one JSON object per line, in JSON Lines:

@verbatim
build/content-index/<package>.jsonl
@end verbatim

@section Rebuilding it

@kindex C-c h i
@findex heroiclands-index-rebuild

@kbd{C-c h i} (@code{heroiclands-index-rebuild}) regenerates it and
reports what it wrote:

@verbatim
sohl -> build/content-index/sohl.jsonl (1606 notes, 1806 KiB)
@end verbatim

It runs @command{npx content-build content-index} from the project root.
The same command works in any terminal.

@section What to expect of it

The index is @strong{derived and disposable}.  It lives under
@file{build/}, is gitignored, and nothing may be authored against it.
Rebuilding costs a frontmatter parse rather than a build — under a second
over sixteen hundred notes — which is why it is a command of its own and
why it need never be committed.

It is also @strong{byte-stable}: rebuilding over an unchanged tree
produces an identical file.  A rebuild is never noise.

@strong{Nothing rebuilds it automatically.}  A note added since the last
rebuild is invisible to completion, to link following, and to
normalization, all of which read the index rather than the tree.  If
something you know exists cannot be found, rebuild first.

@node Writing Wikilinks
@chapter Writing Wikilinks

@cindex wikilinks
@cindex links, wiki

A wikilink names a note by its @emph{address} — @code{being-aurochs},
which is its type and shortcode — and optionally displays something
friendlier after a @code{|}.  The address is the half that has to be
exactly right, and the half nobody can remember.

@menu
* Completion::          Three modes, chosen by what you type.
* Normalization::       What typing @code{]]} does.
* Following a Link::    Jumping to the note, or to a section.
@end menu

@node Completion
@section Completion

@findex heroiclands-goto-capf

Typing @code{[[} opens completion.  What it offers depends on what you
type next.

@table @asis
@item After a @code{#}
The anchors that note declares — exactly those, since the index holds its
whole set.

@item A type and a separator
@code{weapongear-}, @code{being/}, or the package-qualified
@code{sohl-doc-} reads as the start of an address, and the addresses
under it are offered.

@item Anything else
Treated as a @strong{name}.  This is the mode you want most of the time:
you know a note is called ``Bactrian Camel'' and have no idea its
shortcode is @code{bctrncml}.
@end table

Each candidate shows the address and the readable name together:

@verbatim
weapongear-cbw100 — Composite Bow 100
being-bctrncml — Bactrian Camel
armorgear-k34hlm — Kûrbúl ¾-Helm (Kurbul 3/4-Helm)
Killer Whale — being-orca  [being -> Orca]
@end verbatim

The name is part of what gets matched, not a decoration beside it, so
typing @samp{bactrian} finds the note.  Where a name is written in the
setting's orthography, its typeable form is shown in parentheses —
@samp{kurbul} matches.  Aliases get candidates of their own, naming the
note they lead to.

Only the address is ever inserted.

@node Normalization
@section Normalization

Typing @code{]]} closes the link and rewrites it into canonical form:

@verbatim
[[Aurochs]]                -> [[being-aurochs|Aurochs]]
[[aurochs]]                -> [[being-aurochs|Aurochs]]
[[Kurbul 3/4-Helm]]        -> [[armorgear-k34hlm|Kûrbúl ¾-Helm]]
[[Killer Whale]]           -> [[being-orca|Killer Whale]]
[[being-aurochs#dossier]]  -> [[being-aurochs#dossier|Aurochs]]
@end verbatim

You get the address you must write, with the name you actually want
displayed.

@strong{An alias keeps your wording.}  @code{[[Killer Whale]]} becomes
@code{|Killer Whale}, not @code{|Orca}.  If you reached for a sobriquet
you meant that sobriquet; normalization fixes the half that has to be an
address and leaves your prose alone.  A link that already states its own
display half is left alone entirely.

@subsection When it cannot

Anything that is not exactly one note is an error, raised where you typed
it rather than left to fail in a build:

@table @asis
@item No such note
@code{[[being-nosuchthing]]} — @emph{No note named or addressed
`being-nosuchthing'}

@item Ambiguous
@code{[[Alchemy]]} — @emph{`Alchemy' names 2 notes (doc-alchemy,
mysticalability-alch) — say which}

Two notes really are called Alchemy.  Pick one from completion, or type
its address.

@item Unknown anchor
@code{[[being-aurochs#nope]]} — @emph{declares no anchor `nope' (it has:
appearance, dossier)}

The index knows the note's whole anchor set, so this is a fact rather
than a failed search.
@end table

@subsection Only while typing

@vindex heroiclands-goto-canonicalize-on-close

Completion and normalization both apply only to a link you are
@emph{entering}.  They arm when you type @code{[[} and disarm when the
link closes.  Going back to an existing link and editing it wakes
neither, so a link you settled stays settled and a deliberately odd one
is never second-guessed.

Set @code{heroiclands-goto-canonicalize-on-close} to @code{nil} to turn
the rewriting off entirely.

@node Following a Link
@section Following a Link

@kindex C-c h .
@findex heroiclands-goto-follow

@kbd{C-c h .} on a wikilink opens the note it names.  If the link names
an anchor, it lands on that heading's line rather than at the top of the
file.

@kbd{C-c h ,} comes back.  It uses the same marker stack as @kbd{M-.} and
@kbd{M-,}, so the two compose.

A link naming an unknown note, or an anchor the note does not declare,
says so and lists the anchors that do exist.

@node Content Tables
@chapter Content Tables

@cindex dataview
@cindex tables, content

A content note declares the tables it wants with a fenced
@code{dataview} query, and the build fills in the rows.  Obsidian used to
render those live while authoring; this does the same.

@kindex C-c h d
@findex heroiclands-dataview-mode

@kbd{C-c h d} toggles previews in the current note; @kbd{C-c h D}
re-renders them.

Each table appears as an @strong{overlay} below its block.  The buffer is
never modified, so a preview cannot be saved into the note by accident.
The buffer's current text is sent to the renderer, so you can edit a
query and re-render without saving, and a query the expander refuses
shows its error in place.

@vindex heroiclands-dataview-max-rows

It renders through the build's own expander, so a preview cannot disagree
with what actually ships.  Previews are truncated at
@code{heroiclands-dataview-max-rows} (40); set it to @code{nil} for every
row.

@node Querying Content
@chapter Querying Content

@kindex C-c h I
@findex heroiclands-index-query

@kbd{C-c h I} runs a jq filter against the index and shows the results in
a buffer.  Each record is a note's own frontmatter, so a query reads
exactly what the note writes.

@section Recipes

Beings in a category:

@verbatim
select(.type == "being" and .sohl.kbcat == "animal") | .shortcode
@end verbatim

Notes missing something they should have:

@verbatim
select(.type == "being" and (.sohl.kbcat | not)) | .file.path
@end verbatim

What a stale query would have matched — the check that would have caught
eight dead Bestiary tables:

@verbatim
select(.type == "creature") | .file.path
@end verbatim

Every anchor a note declares, with its line:

@verbatim
select(.address.slug == "doc-crafting")
  | .anchors[] | "\(.line)  \(.slug)"
@end verbatim

From a terminal, the same thing:

@verbatim
jq -r 'select(.type == "being") | .name.full' \
  build/content-index/sohl.jsonl
@end verbatim

@node Record Format
@chapter Record Format

Each line is a note's whole frontmatter, unflattened — you address
@code{sohl.body.weight.base} because that is what the note says — plus
these derived fields.  A note that tries to author one of them is a build
error rather than a silent overwrite.

@table @code
@item package
The package the tree compiles as.

@item file
Where the note sits: @code{path}, @code{folder}, @code{name}.  The path
is relative to the content root, never absolute.

@item address
@code{slug} is what goes inside @code{[[@dots{}]]} within the package
(@code{being-aurochs}); @code{canonical} is the package-qualified key
(@code{sohl-being-aurochs}).  Both @code{null} for a note with no type or
shortcode, which has no address at all.

@item anchors
Every @code{@{#slug@}} heading the body declares, each with its
@code{name}, @code{level}, @code{line} in the file, and the
@code{slug#anchor} @code{link} that reaches it.

@item nameAscii
@code{name.full} reduced to printable 7-bit ASCII.  Transliterated, not
stripped: @code{Kûrbúl ¾-Helm} becomes @code{Kurbul 3/4-Helm},
@code{Ærling} becomes @code{AErling}, @code{Þorn} becomes @code{Thorn}.

@item aliasesAscii
The same for @code{name.aliases}, in the authored order.  An empty array
when a note has none — never @code{null}.
@end table

@node Handlebars Helpers
@chapter Handlebars Helpers

@cindex handlebars
@cindex templates

Inside a @file{.hbs} template, typing @code{@{@{} completes across 47
helpers, annotated with their source and with real documentation pulled
from the definitions:

@itemize
@item 15 @strong{sohl}, from @file{src/utils/handlebars-helpers.ts} and
@file{src/sohl.ts}
@item 26 @strong{foundry}, from Foundry's @code{registerHelper} block,
with @code{[DEPRECATED]} flagged
@item 6 @strong{builtin} — Handlebars' own keywords
@end itemize

@code{@{@{#} and @code{@{@{/} put the block helpers (@code{each},
@code{if}, @code{unless}, @code{with}) first.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .82
@item @kbd{C-c h H} @tab Describe a helper.
@item @kbd{C-c h R} @tab Rescan the sources and rebuild the table.
@end multitable

Names are @strong{read from the source}, never restated — add a helper to
@file{src/}, press @kbd{C-c h R}, and it appears.  Foundry's module lives
on an external volume; if it is unmounted the cached Foundry entries are
kept.

@node Special Characters
@chapter Special Characters

@cindex special characters
@cindex accents
@cindex em dash

@file{assets/content} holds 5,916 non-ASCII characters, 4,498 of them em
dashes, so typing them has to be easy.

@section macOS modifier keys

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .72
@item @strong{Left} Option @tab @code{Meta} — @kbd{M-x}, @kbd{M-:},
@kbd{M-w}.
@item @strong{Right} Option @tab Itself: the macOS character layer.
@item Cmd @tab @code{Super} — @kbd{s-z} undo, @kbd{s-=} bigger font.
@item @key{ESC} @tab A sticky Meta: @key{ESC} then @kbd{x} is @kbd{M-x}.
Always works.
@end multitable

Right Option stays a normal Option @strong{deliberately}: with both
Options bound to Meta, none of those 5,916 characters could be typed.

@section Bindings that work on any keyboard

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .3 .2 .3
@item @kbd{C-c u -} @tab — em dash @tab @kbd{C-c u .} @tab … ellipsis
@item @kbd{C-c u n} @tab – en dash @tab @kbd{C-c u x} @tab × multiply
@item @kbd{C-c u '} @tab ’ right quote @tab @kbd{C-c u m} @tab − minus
@item @kbd{C-c u "} @tab ” right dquote @tab @kbd{C-c u l} @tab £ pound
@item @kbd{C-c u d} @tab ° degree @tab @kbd{C-c u >} @tab → arrow
@item @kbd{C-c u s} @tab § section @tab @tab
@end multitable

@kbd{C-c u @key{RET}} inserts anything else by name or hex — @kbd{2014
@key{RET}}, or @kbd{EM DASH @key{RET}}.  @kbd{C-x 8 @key{RET}} is the
same command.

@section Accented letters

The corner key labelled @code{ctrl/opt} on the MX Keys S @emph{is} right
Option, so the standard dead-key sequences work for most accents.

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .45 .35
@headitem Want @tab Right-Option sequence @tab In content
@item á Á é í ú ý @tab @kbd{Opt-e} then the letter @tab 177
@item û â î @tab @kbd{Opt-i} then the letter @tab 78
@item ü ë @tab @kbd{Opt-u} then the letter @tab 20
@item è @tab @kbd{Opt-`} then the letter @tab 10
@item ç @tab @kbd{Opt-c} @tab 2
@end multitable

@strong{Macron is the exception} — macOS has no Option sequence for it at
all, and this content uses 23.  So it is bound directly:

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .3 .2 .3
@item @kbd{C-c u = a} @tab ā @tab @kbd{C-c u = o} @tab ō
@item @kbd{C-c u = i} @tab ī @tab @kbd{C-c u = u} @tab ū
@item @kbd{C-c u = A} @tab Ā @tab @kbd{C-c u = e} @tab ē
@end multitable

Also @kbd{C-c u A} gives Æ and @kbd{C-c u a} gives æ.  Anything else:
@kbd{C-c u @key{RET}}.

@node Housekeeping
@chapter Housekeeping

@section Format on save

Prettier runs on save — asynchronously, so it never blocks — for
@file{ts}, @file{tsx}, @file{js}, @file{mjs}, @file{json}, @file{css},
@file{markdown}, @file{yaml}, @file{hbs} and @file{html}, using the
project's own Prettier and configuration.

@section Occasional rebuilds

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@item @kbd{C-c h i} @tab Content index, after adding notes.
@item @kbd{C-c h R} @tab Handlebars helper table, after adding a helper.
@item @kbd{C-c h M} @tab Link manifests, after they change on disk.
@item @code{M-x my/install-grammars} @tab Reinstall tree-sitter grammars.
@end multitable

Font and size knobs are the two @code{defvar}s at the top of
@file{init.el}.

@section Rebuilding this manual

@verbatim
makeinfo -o ~/.config/emacs/info/heroiclands.info \
  ~/.config/emacs/doc/heroiclands.texi
@end verbatim

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item A note I just added is not offered
Rebuild the index: @kbd{C-c h i}.  Nothing rebuilds it for you.

@item Completion does not open in a link
It only arms when you @emph{type} @code{[[}.  Editing an existing link
deliberately does not wake it.  Retype the link, or use @kbd{C-c h .} to
navigate instead.

@item ``No content index built''
Run @kbd{C-c h i} once in this project.

@item Following a link says the note is unknown
Either the index is stale (@kbd{C-c h i}) or the address really is wrong.
Completion tells you which, by whether it can offer it.

@item An anchor I added is not found
Anchors come from the index too.  Rebuild.

@item Everything is silent in this buffer
The content machinery only runs in a markdown buffer inside a project
carrying @file{package-build.config.yaml}.

@item A helper I just wrote does not complete
@kbd{C-c h R} rescans the sources.

@item Emacs feels cold — no manifests, no language server
You are probably in a rival instance.  Quit it and attach to the daemon
with @strong{Emacs Client}.  @xref{Starting Emacs}.
@end table

